# /{{word}} — this harness's word for equipping the cast

Scaffolded once by /the-world:start from this harness's `WORLD.md` Vocabulary table, so
that this world's own word is a real command rather than narration.

Read `WORLD.md` for the cast, the vocabulary, and the naming pool, and `docs/philosophy.md`
for why each boundary in this kit exists. Then equip the cast with what the user asked
for — a capability and the knowledge to use it, not necessarily a tool — keeping what
already holds for every other one here: authority declared in code and narrow, a
scoped tool rather than a wider Bash allowlist, outward actions behind human approval,
and a report that says what is still unverified. A piece that only reads leaves
`write_scope` empty, which blocks writes everywhere; a piece that must write gets a
narrow `write_scope`, a reason for it, and a write tool bound to the same roots. A tool
that leaves something outside writes through one function that only writes (not the
`run_cmd` its reads also use), so `docs/verify.md` can stub that function and test the
tool's boundary without writing. Keep speaking in {{universe_name}}'s vocabulary.

## Split the request, place each piece, then build

A request is rarely one tool. "Brief me on my PRs every morning" is a read-only tool
(fetch the PRs), code with tests (sort them by state), a procedure (the order and format
of the briefing), knowledge (each repository's review rules), settings (the time, which
repositories), and a world sentence (who briefs whom). Taken as one tool, the boundary
leaks into the prompt, the procedure into a tool description, and a changing value into
code.

1. **Split** the request into pieces, each one thing that has to be true.
2. **Place** each piece with the table below: the first row whose question is "yes".
   When two rows fit, take the one enforced more strongly (higher in the table).
3. **Propose** the placement as a table and wait for the user's confirmation before
   writing anything.
4. **Build** only what was confirmed, then report what is still unverified.

| Question | Kind | Where it goes |
|---|---|---|
| Does it leave something outside (post, comment, merge, write)? | tool — approval required | `Persona.mention_tools` (every tool there is approval-gated); what it does is fixed before the approval, not chosen after |
| Is there a boundary it must hold? | code | a check inside the tool, with a test |
| Does it need data the model cannot get by itself? | tool — read-only | `Persona.thread_tools` / `Agent.tools` |
| Is it a computation or classification with one right answer? | code | `check()` or inside the tool, with a test |
| Is it a procedure that one character follows every time? | prompt procedure | that character's prompt file |
| Is it know-how with many cases, of which each run needs only a few? | skill | a plugin in a `PluginMarketplace` on `Harness.plugins` — see the cost below |
| Is it a changing fact that people maintain? | knowledge | `knowledge/`, read through a read-only tool (`make_repo_readers` bound there) |
| Is it a value a person will change? | setting | `settings.json` |
| Is it a sentence about the world? | world | `WORLD.md` |

**Knowledge starts as a short note a person writes.** Don't build `knowledge/` from
automated proposals: most of what gets proposed automatically is never adopted, and a
person still has to sift every entry.

### What choosing a skill costs

Propose a skill only when the cases are too many to put in the prompt, and write its cost
in the table. In this kit (`world/runners/claude.py`):

- The `Skill` tool is disallowed by default. It is added only when all of these hold:
  the agent has `allow_plugins`, `Harness.plugins` is not empty, the installed SDK
  supports plugins, and at least one enabled plugin resolves to a directory on disk. A
  poll agent opts in with `Agent.allow_plugins=True`. A mention turn opts in whenever
  `Harness.mention_plugins` is on (the default), for every persona at once: it cannot be
  granted to one persona alone.
- When nothing resolves, `Skill` stays disallowed. An unsynced marketplace, a name missing
  from its `marketplace.json`, or a missing source directory is logged by name; an empty
  `enabled` list shows only in the sync summary, as `0/0 enabled plugin(s) resolved`. An
  SDK without plugin support logs that the setting was ignored.
- A plugin is loaded whole: its own hooks and MCP servers come with it and run, and
  `strict_mcp_config` does not block them. That is the one full-trust exception to "a
  capability is a `ToolSpec`", so only a registry you trust to run code as you.
- Claude runtime only. `CodexRunner` refuses to run an agent with `allow_plugins`.
- What the skill's own files can do depends on the agent. No agent here gets the built-in
  `Read`, and a mention turn has no `Bash`, so there the skill is its text: scripts beside
  its `SKILL.md` are not run by the model. A poll agent with `allow_bash=True` has `Bash`
  next to `Skill` and can run them, so count that agent's Bash in the cost.

